  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\n**** Mid-Week Rainfall ****\n\nAfter a period of tranquil weather with high temperatures in the\n60s today and Tuesday, a storm system will come into the picture\non Wednesday. 00z Nov 9 models are all in good agreement\nconcerning the strength/timing of the system, with all solutions\nshowing two short-wave troughs phasing into a significant upper\ntrough east of the Mississippi River. The corresponding surface\nlow will track eastward and deepen over central Illinois on\nWednesday, leading to the next chance for precipitation. With\nstrong upper dynamics in place and GFS precipitable water \nvalues increasing to around 1 inch ahead of the low, confidence\nis growing that showers will become widespread during the\nafternoon and evening...which is reflected in NBM PoPs of\n50-60%. In addition, a narrow band of instability characterized\nby MUCAPEs of 400-800J/kg will support the potential for a few\nrumbles of thunder as well. Once the low passes to the east, the\nbulk of the showers will push into the Ohio River Valley\nWednesday night. After that, attention will turn to the\nincreasingly cyclonic flow aloft and a band of lake-enhanced\nshowers from Lake Michigan on Thursday. The end result will be a\nchilly/gloomy day on Thursday with a stiff N/NE breeze and\nperhaps a few light showers. Storm total rainfall for Wednesday\nand Thursday has increased slightly from previous\nthinking...with the latest NBM indicating a 30-50% chance of\nexceeding 0.25.\n\n\n**** End of Week Cool-Down ****\n\nAs has been advertised for the past several days, a prominent\nupper trough will dig over the Great Lakes/Ohio River Valley by\nthe middle and end of the week. As colder air flows southward on\nthe back side of departing low pressure, temperatures will\ntemporarily drop below normal for this time of year. The 00z\nGFS/ECMWF/GEM all show 850mb temps bottoming out at -4 to -7C by\nFriday. After a breezy/chilly day on Thursday with highs in the\nupper 40s and lower 50s, the coldest day will be Friday when\nhighs only reach the lower to middle 40s. As high pressure\nbuilds into the region and winds diminish, the coldest night of\nthe forecast period will arrive Friday night when lows dip into\nthe middle to upper 20s across the board. Once the upper trough\nshifts eastward and upper heights begin to rise, temperatures\nwill moderate early next week...with readings climbing back into\nthe lower 60s by next Monday.",
